title: Coagulation Disorder
---

# Coagulation disorder

- **Etiology of cogulopathies:**
  - **Congenital**
    - **X-linked** - **Haemophilia A** (Factor VIII deficiency), **Haemophilia B** (Factor IX deficiency)
    - **Autosomal** - **Von Willebrand disease** (most common inherited bleeding disorder), rare coagulation factor deficiencies (II, V, VII, X, XI, XIII), Dysfibrinogenaemia, Hypofibrinogenaemia
  - **Acquired**
    - **Vitamin K deficiency** - Cholestasis, malnutrition, ABx use
    - **Drug-induced coagulopathy** - Warfarin, Heparin, LMWH, fondaparinux, DOACs
    - **Under production**
      - Decompensated cirrhosis - decreased synthesis of factor V, VII, VIII, IX, X, XI
    - **Excessive consumption**
      - Consumptive coagulopathies - DIC, thrombotic microangiopathies
    - **Acquired inhibitors of coagulation**
      - Acquired haemophilia A - anti-VIII Ab
      - Acquired von Willebrand syndrome (aVWS)

# Clotting Profiles

- **Clotting profiles** - PT (INR), aPTT +/- TT, Fibrinogen, D-dimer
  - **PT** (prothrombin time) - typically reflects factor VII activity and common pathway when exposed to TF
    - Method - measure time for plasma to clot when exposed to TF in vitro
    - INR used to standardized PT assay for therapeutic monitoring (warfarin).
  - **aPTT** (activated partial thromboplastin time) - typically reflects factor VIII, XI activity and common pathway
    - Method - measure time for plasma to clot when exposed to thromboplastic material w/o TF and contact w/ negatively charge material, initiating XII activation in vitro
    - aPTT is used for therapeutic monitoring of heparin therapy.
  - **TT** (thrombin time) - typically a surrogate for fibrinogen (factor I) activity
    - Method - measure time for plasma to clot when exposed to exogenous thrombin in vitro
  - **Fibrinogen** - terminal coagulation factor
    - Precautions - Acute phase reactant (prone to false +ve)
    - Indications for measurement - when suspecting fibrinogen deficiency (liver disease, rare cause of hypofibrinogenemia), or consumptive coagulopathy (DIC, TMA)
  - **D-dimer** - a fibrin degradation product after cleavage by plasmin
    - Indications for measurement - a marker for thrombosis
- Additional tests
  - 1:1 Mixing test - differentiation between clotting factor deficiency or acquired coagulation inhibitor
    - Principles - 50% of clotting factor activity sufficient for normal clotting time
    - Procedure - 1:1 mixing of patient plasma
  - Specific clotting factor assay
  - vWF assay

# Approach to Isolated Prolonged PT

- **Isolated prolonged PT** - indicative of factor VII problem
- **DDx of isolated prolonged PT:**
  - Overwarfarinization (factor II, VII, IX, X inhibition)
  - Vitamin K deficiency (factor II, VII, IX, X) - Cholestasis or malnutrition
  - Decompensated cirrhosis (reduced synthesis)
  - Inherited factor VII deficiency
- **Evaluation and Mx:**
  - Repeat and confirm
  - Look for Hx of warfarin use
  - Look for eatures of cholestasis (obstructive jaundice), liver disease and workup thoroughly
  - Treatment:
    - Parenteral vitamin K to correct coagulopathy
    - Overwarfarinization - Parenteral vitamin K + FPP or Prothrombin complex concentrate (concentrate of vitamin K dependent coagulation factors)

# Approach to Isolated Prolonged aPTT

- **DDx of isolated prolonged aPTT:**
  - Drug-induced coagulopathy -Heparin
  - Congenital - Haemophilia A/B, Von Willebrand disease
  - Acquired - Acquired haemophilia A
- **Evaluation and Mx:**
  - Repeat and confirm
  - In the absence of clinical bleeding - consider heparin contamination (blood drawn from catheter) or lupus anticoagulant (APLS), or factor XII deficiency (10% of population)
  - In the presence of clinical bleeding:
    - Inherited causes - Von Willebrand disease (both sex) and haemophilias (men)
    - Acquired causes - Acquired Haemophilia A (both sex)
  - Management:
    - Congenital haemophilia A/B - factor concentrates (plasma derived or recombinant)
    - vWD - DDAVP

# Approach to Prolonged PT and aPTT

- **DDx of prolonged PT and aPTT:**
  - Disseminated intravascular coagulopathy (DIC)
  - Extreme liver decompensation w/ cholestatic component
  - Extreme vitamin K deficiency
  - Overwarfarinization
  - Factor X deficiency and hypo/dys-fibrinogenemia (rare)
- **Evaluation and Mx:**
  - Repeat and confirm
  - Consider and confirm DIC - test for fibrinogen, D-dimer, platelets +/- schistocytes
    - Typical clotting profile (seldom fullblown, requires clinical suspicion) - ↑PT, ↑aPTT, ↓fibrinogen, ↓platelet + schistocytes
  - Management:
    - FFP in active bleeding
    - Treat underlying cause
